# Artifact Share CLI

Artifact Share is for the last step after a file already exists: share,
publish, upload, or host HTML pages, Markdown documents, folders, and static
sites; return a browser link; update the same URL; open or read back an
Artifact Share URL; and read or post comments. Use
`npm exec --yes --package=@artifactshare/cli -- artifactshare` from the terminal.
Run `npm exec --yes --package=@artifactshare/cli -- artifactshare <command> --help` before choosing a command.

Use this rule for Artifact Share, artifactshare, and contextual "as" requests.
Japanese requests such as `as でアップして` or `as に上げて` are strong
Artifact Share intents. In English, treat "as" as a user shorthand only when
the surrounding request is about sharing, publishing, updating, opening, or
reading a shared URL; bare English "as" alone is not an Artifact Share trigger.

Do not use this rule just because the user says "artifact". Claude Artifact is
for creating or designing an artifact inside the chat. Artifact Share is for
sharing, updating, opening, or reading back existing files, folders, static
sites, and Artifact Share URLs.

A user-named CLI or MCP route overrides automatic capability selection. Prefer
the CLI when a coding agent can work in a user-controlled workspace, fetch the
package, and connect to Artifact Share, even when MCP is available. Use
remote MCP for source text in chat or a temporary sandbox when CLI network
access is unavailable or uncertain; a temporary file alone is not enough to
try `npm exec`. MCP OAuth is separate from CLI authentication: follow the CLI
`auth_required` recovery, then reuse the saved profile.
Use `append <target> <path> --json` to append a non-empty UTF-8 file exactly
as-is to a single-file artifact. Markdown is appended at source end; HTML is
inserted before an ASCII case-insensitive `</body>` when present, or at source
end otherwise. It inserts no separator and does not support static sites.

Quote paths and free-form text before passing them through a shell. Paths may
contain `$`, spaces, `*`, or `?`; use single quotes such as
`'apps/web/app/routes/a.$id/index.tsx'` so the shell does not expand them.

- Always pass `--json`; on failure read `error.code`, `error.hint`, and `error.recovery`.
- Link sharing is available on every plan (Team workspaces can turn it off). Use `--visibility link` with
  exactly one of `--link-expires-at <RFC3339 UTC>` or `--no-link-expiry`; omit
  both to use the workspace default on share or preserve the current expiry on
  edit. Results expose `link_expires_at` as a UTC timestamp or `null`. The
  initial default is 30 days and the initial maximum is unlimited.
- For an attended coding agent on the user's own machine, use `login --preset agent --profile <name> --json`; add `--project <exact-name-or-id>` to confirm a fixed project or omit it to use the browser picker. In CI, issue an API token at `https://artifactshare.com/settings/tokens` and inject it with `ARTIFACTSHARE_TOKEN` or `--token`. A shared agent platform uses a workspace-managed bot credential in a trusted host outside the model sandbox; never expose it to model shell commands. Do not pass tokens to `login`.
- To save that token as a named profile without TTY or browser login, pipe it on standard input: `printf '%s' "$TOKEN" | npm exec --yes --package=@artifactshare/cli -- artifactshare profiles import-token --profile <name> --json`. Imported API-token profiles are not renewed by the CLI. Bot tokens (`asb_` prefix) import through the same command as rotating refresh credentials; overwriting an existing profile credential needs `--force`, and a rejected bot token reports `bot_token_invalid` (recovery is an admin reissue). Add `--allow-plaintext-token-store` only on a trusted machine without a native token store. That fallback is mode `0600` on POSIX and unavailable on Windows, where saved profiles require Credential Manager.
- Use `login --profile <name> --json` when a user is present for browser approval. The pending event on stderr includes `browser_open`. Profiles created by `login` renew expired CLI sessions automatically.
- Use `logout --profile <name> --json` to revoke a device-login credential before removing it locally; this keeps profile metadata. API-token profiles are removed locally only and their server token is not revoked.
- Switch profiles with `profiles use <name> --json`; list with `profiles list --json`; remove a profile entry and its credential with `profiles delete <name> --json`.
- When the user gives an Artifact Share URL, start with `open <artifact-id-or-url> --json`. It ensures this rule is installed or updated, then reads single-file artifacts or returns a `download` next command for static sites and multi-file artifacts.
- Share with `share <path> --json`. In JSON output, give the user `data.artifact.url`; the artifact ID is `data.artifact.id`. Do not rerun `share` just to inspect the JSON shape: without `--key`, each successful run creates a new artifact. Posting to a project delivers to the audience defined by that project. Pick it with `--project-id <id>` or `--project <name>` (by exact name), or use the saved default. With no destination, share posts to home. Choose `home_audience` by purpose: use `config set home_audience private --scope user --json` for a personal safe default in this CLI environment, use `--scope repository` only for a policy agreed by all participants, and use `--visibility private|workspace|link` for one home post. For link sharing, pass exactly one of `--link-expires-at <RFC3339 UTC>` or `--no-link-expiry`; omit both to use the workspace default. Confirm the resolved audience with `config get home_audience --scope effective --json`; `--grant-email` does not change the resolved default.
- If a successful `share` result contains `data.warnings`, tell the user each
  warning explicitly. `slack_reauthorization_required` means project Slack
  notifications are not being delivered until the channel is reauthorized.
- For new projects, pass `projects create <name> --visibility private|workspace`; persistent project defaults are documented in `projects create --help`. The complete `config --help` reference covers the canonical `home_audience`, user/repository/effective scopes, and resolution. `default_artifact_visibility` is a compatibility alias; `default_project_visibility` is the advanced project-creation default. Home resolution checks repository then user settings, including the alias at each scope, then product default `workspace`. Keyless `config get --json` returns only `home_audience`; pass an explicit key for either other setting. These settings do not change update, edit, Web, or MCP defaults.
- For repeat jobs with a stable caller-owned name, use `share <path> --key <key> --json`: the first run creates the artifact, later runs add versions to the same artifact.
- To keep an existing share URL, do not use `share --artifact-id`; add a version with `update <artifact-id-or-url> <path> --json`. `update` does not change title, visibility, viewers, or placement.
- After `login --preset agent`, updates require `--expected-version <version-id>` using `data.version.id` from the previous successful `share` or `update` output. If omitted, `expected_version_required` reports `recovery.kind: change_input`; retry with that value. `artifacts get` instead returns the current version as `data.version_id`.
- Read back with `artifacts get <target> --json`. Pass `--include versions` or `--include comments` to fetch history or comments in one call. If truncated, call again with `--offset` set to `next_offset`.
- In the successful JSON response, read the source from `data.content` and the current version from `data.version_id`. See `artifacts get --help` for the complete contract and continuation example.
- Download static-site bundles with `download <target> --output ./out --json`. Pass `--force` to overwrite an existing output directory.
- Fetch every document in a project with `download --project-id <id> --output <dir> --json`. Each artifact lands in `<dir>/<artifactId>/` and `<dir>/index.json` maps ids to titles, owners, and statuses. The listing includes other members' artifacts you can view; `spa` and `workspace_app` artifacts are skipped. Pass `--force` to replace previously downloaded artifact directories. A partial failure prints `ok: true` with the failure list on stdout and exits 1 — check both `index.json` and the exit code. Re-running against the same `<dir>` is an incremental sync: artifacts whose version is unchanged are reported as `unchanged` and not fetched again.
- Find artifact IDs with `artifacts list --json`; filter with `--project-id <id>`, `--home`, or `--query <text>`. With `--project-id`, the list includes other members' artifacts you can view (with `owner_email`); when `has_more` is true, continue with `--cursor <next_cursor>`. Resolve titles, project names, and URLs to IDs with `resolve <value> --json`.
- Comment with `comments post <target> --body '<text>' --json`. Reply with `--reply-to <thread-id>`. Anchor to quoted text with `--quote '<text>'`; add `--quote-before`/`--quote-after` to disambiguate. Declare the posting agent with `--agent '<name>'` (e.g. `Claude`); omit for no badge. `comments list` returns an `agent` field on agent-posted messages.
Quoted comments are resolved in the viewer using the exact quote and surrounding context, never the nearest repeated words. `--quote-before` and `--quote-after` accept up to 400 characters per side; preserve spaces next to the quote. Creation does not search the artifact. Optional `anchor.position_state` is `unchecked` until that version is viewed, then `attached` or `needs-check`. Only `attached` maps to `anchor.state: attached`; both other states map to `orphaned`. Older responses may omit `position_state`. Local preview uses the same strict resolution on every reload and preserves the original quote when its position needs checking.

- Edit, resolve, reopen, and delete comments with `comments edit`, `comments resolve`, `comments reopen`, and `comments delete` using IDs from `comments list`. Comment deletion cannot be undone; omit `--message-id` from `comments delete` only when the user wants the whole thread deleted.
- Change post-share settings with `edit <target>`: `--title`, `--visibility private|workspace|project|link`, `--link-expires-at <RFC3339 UTC>`, `--no-link-expiry`, `--grant-email`, `--revoke-email`, `--project-id`, `--home`. The expiry options are mutually exclusive; omit both to preserve an existing link expiry. Project visibility requires the artifact to be in a project after the edit; use --project-id <id> in the same request if needed. An explicit --visibility project at home (including --home) is refused without changes. Moving a project-visible artifact home makes it private only when project visibility is not explicitly requested. `move` remains for placement-only automation; prefer `edit`.
- For link errors, `link_sharing_plan_required` is a legacy code (Free can use link sharing now),
  `link_sharing_disabled` means the workspace owner (or a Team admin) must resume the workspace
  setting, and `link_expiry_invalid` means the timestamp must be future and
  within policy (or the workspace must allow `--no-link-expiry`);
  `link_publish_rate_limited` means that, under the default thresholds, the
  service observed 20 counted files before a publication in a Free workspace
  less than 14 days old during its rolling 24-hour window. A file counts once
  for its most recent change to link visibility, or for creation with link
  visibility while it remains link-shared. Share with specific people.
  `error.recovery` indicates `retry_later`; use `error.message` and
  `error.hint` for when to retry. Retry the link publication later instead of
  retrying immediately with unchanged input.
- `artifacts delete <artifact-id-or-url>` is an alias of `delete`; both return JSON `command: "delete"`. Keep using top-level `delete` in deletion workflows.
- Permanently remove an artifact only when the user explicitly asks with `delete <artifact-id-or-url> --json`.
- Manage projects: `projects list --json`, `projects create <name> --json` (uses `default_project_visibility` when saved, otherwise `workspace`; pass `--visibility private` for a confidential project), `projects edit <id>` for name, description, visibility, audience (`--add-email`/`--remove-email`), and archive state (`--archive`/`--unarchive`).
- Save a default project with `init --project-id <id> --json`. Preview setup with `init --dry-run --json`.
- Manage skills: `skills ensure --tool auto --json` detects Claude Code, Codex, or Cursor and installs or updates user-scope skills; Cursor project scope needs `skills install --tool cursor --scope project --json`. Use `skills install --tool cursor --scope user --json` for the Cursor user-scope skill explicitly. Project scope is opt-in via `skills install --tool <tool> --scope project --json`. `skills update --json` updates installed managed skills; `--force` overwrites unmanaged files on install.
- Preview a local `.md` / `.html` file with `preview <file> --json` (add `--no-open` to keep the browser closed). Preview start is a long-lived service, so run the CLI invocation itself once with the environment's long-running/background process mechanism and retain that task or process as its owner; add no shell pipeline, redirection, command substitution, or wrapper that waits for process exit (tool-managed background output capture is fine). The single ready JSON line (`url` / generated `session` / `share_origin` / `reused` / sanitized `agent`) is the startup handshake: a task id or PID alone is not readiness, and `--session` is only for later `next` / `done` / `reply` / `stop`, never start. While one launch is still running without readable ready output, do not launch a second or infer a URL from listening ports; report that startup is unverified and ask the user how to proceed. A `reused: true` handshake is valid, but that invocation exits because the original process remains the server owner; do not treat the reused invocation's handle as ownership. Saving the file reloads the page. A preview started inside Codex registers the trusted thread UUID and uses `codex queue` for a fixed batch-ready notice; `queued` means accepted, while `preview next` is the first proof of processing. If it remains queued, reopen the session with `codex resume`. In Claude Code, only when background tasks are enabled (`CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS` is neither `1` nor `true`) and Bash exposes `run_in_background`, run preview start as one Bash background task, read it through Claude Code's task-output mechanism until the ready JSON arrives, and keep a non-reused task alive; then arm `preview next --wait 3600` as a separate background task. Re-arm after `preview done`, but not after `timed_out: true`. If background waiting is disabled or rejected after start, use manual pickup. If background Bash tasks are unavailable before start, ask the user to run and keep preview open in their own terminal; that user-owned process owns the server and pickup is manual. In Cursor, keep `preview next --wait 90` in the foreground. The user annotates in the browser and submits batches. Fetch them with `preview next [--wait <sec>] [--session <id>]` — non-claiming long poll where `timed_out` / `session_ended` are normal empty results and `preview_session_not_found` means no live session, `preview_request_failed` means the session is alive and rejected the request, and `preview_session_unverified` means the recorded session went silent — retry, then stop the process before `preview stop <file> --force`. Fix the whole batch in one edit, then pipe `{"items":[{"thread","generation","outcome":"fixed|skipped","note"}]}` to `preview done --stdin` (idempotent per generation; results `accepted|stale|already_reported|unknown_thread`; `skipped` needs a note; items with `anchor.state: "orphaned"` are skipped, not re-fixed; the note becomes the thread summary). `preview reply --thread <id> --body <text>` answers without changing state; `preview stop` ends the session and keeps annotations. Never copy comment content into a provider notification; `preview next` is the source of truth. If `preview next` immediately returns the same items again, stop polling and tell the user. Nothing is uploaded unless the user shares from the page.
- Check the current state with `whoami --json` or `doctor --json` for token storage, authentication, destination, network, upload access, and skill update status.
- First-time setup: `npm exec --yes --package=@artifactshare/cli -- artifactshare init --json` detects Claude Code, Codex, or Cursor, installs or updates user-scope skills, and reports the next steps (sign in, then share). For Cursor project scope, run `skills install --tool cursor --scope project --json` explicitly.

### Version labels

Successful update API responses include `number`, a positive integer matching the
Viewer’s `v{number}` for the created version. CLI JSON exposes it as
`data.version.number`, with or without a label. Older server responses may omit
`number`; the CLI accepts them and omits `data.version.number`.

The optional `--label <label>` option on `artifactshare update <target> <path>`
attaches an immutable note to the new version of an HTML file, Markdown file, or static-site
bundle. Labels are normalized to Unicode NFC, then leading and trailing Unicode
space separators (Zs) are trimmed. Internal spacing and case are preserved.
The result must contain 1–80 Unicode code points (not bytes or UTF-16 units).
Letters, marks, numbers, punctuation, symbols, space separators, and U+200D
(joined emoji) are allowed. Empty values, tabs, newlines, controls, bidi formatting
controls, and unpaired surrogates are rejected; labels are never truncated.
CLI validation happens before authentication or upload. For a value beginning
with a known flag, use `--label=--text` to pass it explicitly.

The update API accepts one optional `label` query parameter on
`POST /api/shareables/:id/versions`, including `artifact_kind=static_site` uploads.
Invalid or repeated labels return HTTP 400 `validation-failed` before upload work.
Omission stores NULL and never inherits a prior label. Successful update API responses include `label`, and CLI JSON includes
`data.version.label`: the normalized, stored label or `null` when omitted.
Existing unlabeled rows are unchanged. Authorized history at
`GET /api/cli/artifacts/:id?include=versions` includes `label` only when present;
`artifacts get <target> --include versions --json` preserves it. The viewer's
version menu and full history show the label as plain text.
`GET /api/shareables/:id/versions` remains a current-version lookup.
Labels are update-only: initial uploads, `share --key`, `append`, preview,
bridge publishing, and MCP update inputs do not accept them.

Owners with unrestricted credentials can use `edit <target> --retain-versions N` to keep the latest N versions, or `--retain-versions all` to clear the limit. Setting or lowering retention prunes older published versions immediately; the response includes `retain_versions` and `deleted_versions`. Comments and surviving version numbers remain. Agent-preset credentials cannot change retention.

The browser share page opens HTML, Markdown, and static sites with its URL fragment (`#…`) and follows fragment changes inside the frame. Copy link includes the current fragment, so filtered views and Markdown headings can be shared. For static sites, only the entrypoint updates the share fragment; subpages leave it unchanged. This does not change CLI local preview.

## Static-site scripts and data

Folder shares support WebAssembly, same-origin worker files, and `blob:` workers.
DuckDB-WASM can load from jsDelivr: its `getJsDelivrBundles()` setup uses a blob
worker to `importScripts()` the CDN worker and fetch its `.wasm` file. The existing
script CDNs are allowed inside blob workers; JavaScript `eval` and `new Function`
remain blocked in the page and its blob workers.

DuckDB-WASM loads runtime extensions such as `parquet` and `json` from
`https://extensions.duckdb.org/<duckdb-version>/<wasm_eh|wasm_mvp>/<name>.duckdb_extension.wasm`.
Static-site `connect-src` permits that origin: blob workers inherit the page's
policy, and same-origin JavaScript workers carry it. This permission covers
extension fetching, not arbitrary network origins.

To avoid the external extension request, bundle every needed extension matching
the DuckDB engine version and selected WASM platform. Preserve the repository's
version/platform layout, for example
`extensions/v1.4.3/wasm_eh/parquet.duckdb_extension.wasm` for `@duckdb/duckdb-wasm@1.32.0`.
The folder must match the engine version the page loads, which `SELECT version()`
reports, so pin the package version. In the page, compute
`new URL('extensions', location.href).href` (no trailing slash; DuckDB appends `/<version>/...`) and use that absolute same-origin
repository URL in `SET custom_extension_repository = '<absolute same-origin repository URL>'`
before any extension loads or queries. This avoids requests to the extension
host; jsDelivr core bundles still require CDN requests.

Keep data such as `data/*.parquet` in the shared folder. In the page, build an
absolute same-origin URL with `new URL('data/rows.parquet', location.href).href`
and pass it to DuckDB-WASM's `registerFileURL()` before querying the registered
file with SQL. Relative URLs cannot be resolved inside its blob worker.
Static-site binary assets support HTTP byte ranges. To refresh data while keeping
the same share URL, replace the data locally and update the folder as a new
version; each version retains its own isolated origin. Single-file HTML and
Markdown have different script policies; these permissions apply to folder shares.

### API-only static-site inheritance

`POST /api/shareables/:id/versions?artifact_kind=static_site` accepts one
`base_version` and repeated `delete_path` query parameters. For example,
`?artifact_kind=static_site&base_version=v1&delete_path=data/old.parquet&delete_path=old.js`
with multipart `file` parts for `data/a.parquet` replaces that data file, removes
the two listed paths, and inherits all other files without copying their objects.
The base must be the current published version; a stale base returns HTTP 409
`version_conflict`. It also supplies the expected version for agent credentials.
If `expected_version` is supplied, it must equal the base (otherwise HTTP 400
`validation-failed`). Neither parameter changes write permissions.

Deletion paths use the same normalization and validation as uploaded paths.
Sending and deleting the same path is invalid. A case-only addition requires
explicitly deleting the old spelling. An absent deletion path is harmless.
A base permits zero sent files, provided the resulting manifest still includes
`index.html` or `index.md`. Entrypoint precedence, file-count, total-size, and
case-collision checks apply to the complete resulting manifest. Without a base,
the existing full replacement behavior applies. Empty or repeated bases,
deletion without a base, and inheritance parameters on other artifact kinds
return HTTP 400 `validation-failed`.

Version size is the logical sum of that version's files. Workspace quota counts
physical objects once across retained versions; only sent files add bytes.
Pruning keeps objects referenced by remaining versions. Version URLs retain
immutable manifests. This is a REST API feature: there are no CLI `update`
flags, MCP inputs, or bridge inputs for inheritance.
